    id 43
    label "iWWF5nN7fUA"
    step 0
    date "2017-05-26T16:49:19Z"
    title "Century-Old Drug Tested in Boys with Autism"
    description "Suramin is a 100-year-old drug developed to treat African sleeping sickness. It is not approved for any therapeutic use in the U.S. However, a small clinical trial at UC San Diego School of Medicine found that a single intravenous dose of suramin produced measurable, but transient, improvements in five boys with autism spectrum disorder (ASD). More broadly, the trial findings support the &#8220;cell danger response theory,&#8221; which posits that autism is driven by metabolic dysfunction &#8212; and thus treatable. Larger, longer clinical trials are needed to assess suramin as an ASD treatment. &#10;&#10;In this video, we hear from lead researcher Robert Naviaux, MD, PhD, and the mothers of two boys who received suramin in the trial. &#10;&#10;For more information about Suramin and Autism, visit: &#10;https://health.ucsd.edu/suramin"
    channeltitle "UC San Diego Health"
    channelid "UCOaegTYfii7Gl9ytqZa8i_Q"
